#nop ── warrior.tin ── read while the warrior's session is current ──────
#nop
#nop A class file is bound to a session by *when* it is read, not by anything
#nop written in it: $session is whichever session judytin is focused on at
#nop the time, so crew.tin does `#session war ...` and then `#read` this, and
#nop every setting below lands on the warrior alone.
#nop
#nop Class files set keyed variables and nothing else. Actions and functions
#nop are keyed by name and global to the client, so one defined here would
#nop belong to all four characters. core.tin owns those and branches on
#nop $role[$session].

#variable {role[$session]}      {warrior}
#variable {atk[$session]}       {kill}
#variable {atkspell[$session]}  {}
#variable {heal[$session]}      {}
#variable {buffspell[$session]} {}

#nop Wimpy is the game's own auto-flee and beats anything a client can do:
#nop it fires inside the combat round rather than after it. The warrior tanks,
#nop so it stays in far longer than the people standing behind it.
#variable {wimpy[$session]}     {12}
#variable {isleader[$session]}    {yes}
